course: history
lesson: 46
title: The struggle of 1917–21 and the Holodomor
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: When the Russian Empire collapsed in 1917, Ukrainians in Kyiv set up a council, the Central Rada, and in January 1918 declared an independent Ukrainian People's Republic; the teacher had said Ukraine «had no state before 1991», and Danylo was right that it had one, even if it lasted only a few years.
explain: Those years were like a playground where five bigger kids fight over one ball at once: Bolsheviks, White Russian armies, Ukrainian governments, Polish troops and peasant armies like Nestor Makhno's all fought over the land (the Bolsheviks were Lenin's Communists, who had taken power in Russia), and in the end most of Ukraine became a Soviet republic.
explain: Danylo's stories made heroes and villains simple, but the real people were mixed: Hetman Skoropadsky depended on German troops yet founded the Academy of Sciences, and Petliura is honoured by many while historians still argue about pogroms (violent attacks on Jews) by troops under his command.
explain: In 1932–1933 Soviet policy took grain and food from the villages and stopped hungry peasants from leaving, and millions died of hunger; Ukrainians call it the Holodomor, «killing by hunger», and every November families light a candle in a window to remember.
explain: The wise lesson: the truth is terrible enough without bigger numbers, and you can name a famine as man-made, say that many countries call it genocide, and still say which points historians debate.
fact: After the Russian revolution of 1917 the Central Rada in Kyiv, led by Mykhailo Hrushevsky, proclaimed the Ukrainian People's Republic in November 1917 and full independence on 22 January 1918, though the country was split and many forces fought over it.
fact: At Kruty in January 1918 a few hundred defenders, among them student volunteers, fought a much larger Bolshevik force; many young men died, the advance was delayed only briefly, and the battle became a symbol of sacrifice.
fact: In April 1918 Pavlo Skoropadsky took power as Hetman with German support; his Hetmanate founded the Ukrainian Academy of Sciences, which still exists, and fell in December 1918.
fact: On 22 January 1919 the Ukrainian People's Republic and the West Ukrainian People's Republic (in Galicia, with Lviv) proclaimed unity in Kyiv, but war split them again; 22 January is now celebrated as the Day of Unity.
fact: Symon Petliura led the Ukrainian People's Republic's army and government in 1919–1920; in the civil war many forces carried out pogroms against Jews, including troops nominally (on paper) under his command, and historians still debate his responsibility.
fact: Nestor Makhno led an anarchist (against any government) peasant army in southern Ukraine from 1918 to 1921, used the tachanka, a horse cart with a machine gun, fought several sides and was finally defeated by the Bolsheviks; he died in Paris.
fact: In the 1920s Soviet «Ukrainisation» let Ukrainian schools, newspapers and theatre grow fast; in the 1930s it was reversed and many writers were shot, a generation called the «Executed Renaissance».
fact: In 1932–1933 millions died in Soviet Ukraine in a famine caused by Soviet policy: forced collectivisation (making farmers give their land and animals to big state farms), impossible grain quotas (amounts each village had to hand over), seizing food and blocking starving peasants from leaving; Ukraine and many other countries recognise it as genocide (an attempt to destroy a people), while some historians debate the legal label.
fact: Population experts (demographers) estimate about 3.5 to 5 million Holodomor deaths in Soviet Ukraine; older figures like 7 or 10 million are not supported by most demographers.
fact: In 1937–1938, in the Great Terror (also called the purges), the Soviet secret police arrested and shot huge numbers of people across the USSR; in Ukraine writers, teachers, priests and Communist leaders were among them, and many lie in the mass graves at Bykivnia near Kyiv.
fact: Between 1919 and 1939 Galicia (with Lviv) and Volhynia were in Poland, Bukovyna in Romania and Transcarpathia in Czechoslovakia; Ukrainians there had parties and cooperatives but were pressed to assimilate (give up their own language and ways), and Carpatho-Ukraine declared independence for one day in March 1939.
exercise: What did the Central Rada proclaim on 22 January 1918? | Full independence of the Ukrainian People's Republic | The end of the Cossack Sich and all its old laws and freedoms | The union with Poland | The baptism of Rus
exercise: Who led the Central Rada? | Historian Mykhailo Hrushevsky | Hetman Ivan Mazepa from Baturyn | Nestor Makhno and his peasant army | The poet Taras Shevchenko himself
exercise: Who were among the defenders at Kruty in 1918? | Student volunteers | Cossacks of the Sich | Roman legions in armour | French sailors from Odesa
exercise: What did the battle of Kruty really do? | It delayed the Bolshevik advance only briefly | It stopped the Bolshevik army for good and saved Kyiv for many years | It ended the First World War | It freed Lviv from Poland
exercise: Which institution did Skoropadsky's Hetmanate found? | The Ukrainian Academy of Sciences | The Kyiv-Mohyla Academy, the oldest school in Kyiv | The Ostroh Academy | The United Nations
exercise: Whose troops kept Skoropadsky in power? | German troops | Swedish troops | British troops | Troops from Mongolia
exercise: Which two republics proclaimed unity on 22 January 1919? | The Ukrainian People's Republic and the West Ukrainian People's Republic | Ukraine and Poland | The Ukrainian People's Republic and the Russian Soviet Republic of Lenin's Bolsheviks | Galicia and Romania
exercise: Why do historians still argue about Symon Petliura? | Troops nominally under his command carried out pogroms against Jews | He never existed | He wrote the Ems decree of 1876, which banned printing books and plays in Ukrainian | He sold Crimea
exercise: What was a tachanka? | A horse cart with a machine gun | A long Cossack boat with oars and sails | A kind of borscht cooked with beans | A silver coin minted by the Hetmanate
exercise: How did Makhno's struggle end? | The Bolsheviks defeated him and he died in Paris | He became tsar | He won every battle and ruled southern Ukraine until he was very old | He founded Odesa
exercise: What happened to Ukrainian culture in the 1920s Soviet Ukraine? | Ukrainian schools and newspapers grew fast | Ukrainian was banned from the very first day of Soviet rule | Only Latin was allowed | Nothing changed at all
exercise: What does «Executed Renaissance» mean? | A generation of Ukrainian writers shot in the 1930s | A style of painting that came back into fashion in Italy | A Cossack festival | A law of 1876
exercise: What caused the Holodomor of 1932–1933? | Soviet policy: grain seizures, quotas and blocking peasants from leaving | Only a long, dry summer with no rain, which no government could have stopped | A plague of locusts | A war with Germany
exercise: What do many demographers estimate as the Holodomor death toll? | About 3.5 to 5 million | About ten thousand people | Exactly 20 million, no more | Fewer than a hundred people
exercise: What is Bykivnia? | A place near Kyiv with mass graves of Terror victims | An old Cossack fortress on an island in the Dnipro below the rapids | A river in Lviv | A Ukrainian dish
exercise: Which country held Transcarpathia between the wars? | Czechoslovakia | The Kingdom of Hungary | The German Republic | The Kingdom of Romania
exercise: Which country held Lviv between the two world wars? | Poland | Romania | The Soviet Union | Austria
test: Danylo says Ukraine went independent in 1917 and everybody agreed. What's more exact? | It declared independence in 1918, but many armies fought over the country | Yes, everybody agreed and peace came at once to every town and village | Ukraine never declared anything before 1991 | Russia gave it freedom as a present
test: Teacher says «no Ukrainian state before 1991». What should Danylo show? | The Ukrainian People's Republic of 1917–1921, short-lived but real | The Roman Empire map | Nothing, the teacher's right, Ukraine was always only a part of somebody else's state | A Eurovision trophy
test: «300 kids stopped the entire Bolshevik army at Kruty, end of story» — fix it. | A few hundred fought bravely, delayed the advance briefly, and many died | They destroyed the whole army, and the Bolsheviks never came back to Ukraine again | Nobody fought at Kruty | Kruty was in France
test: Danylo goes «Skoropadsky was just a German puppet, full stop». Wise reply? | He relied on Germans but also founded lasting institutions | Yes, he did nothing at all and nothing he made outlived him | He was a Swedish king | He beat the Germans single-handed
test: If the 1919 Act of Unity had lasted, where would Lviv have been in the 1930s? | In a united Ukrainian state, not in Poland | In Canada | In the Ottoman Empire | Under the sea
test: Danylo says Petliura was a pure hero, no questions allowed. What would a historian say? | Many honour him, but the pogroms debate is real and should be named | Questions about history are forbidden, because heroes must never be doubted | He was only a villain, nothing else | He lived in ancient Rome
test: in a cartoon Makhno beats everyone with his cart-gun and lives happy ever after. fix? | He fought several sides but lost to the Bolsheviks and died abroad | That's exactly what happened | He never had any carts | He became president of France
test: Danylo says the Soviets banned Ukrainian from day one. What's the full story? | In the 1920s it grew, then in the 1930s it was reversed and writers were shot | Yes, banned from day one, nothing in Ukrainian was ever printed in Soviet times | It was never touched at all | Only the Pope banned it
test: «10 million died in the Holodomor, everyone knows that», Danylo says. What's the honest move? | Use the newer estimates of about 3.5 to 5 million; the truth is terrible enough | Say 20 million, it sounds stronger | Say nobody died | Refuse to talk about it
test: Teacher calls it «a famine in the USSR». What did he add to be truthful? | That it was man-made and many countries call it genocide | That it was caused only by weather | That it happened in Brazil | That it never really hurt anyone
test: Danylo says the 1937–38 purges only hit Ukrainians. What's fair? | All Soviet peoples suffered; Ukraine was hit very hard | Only Ukrainians were touched | Nobody was ever arrested | Only Germans were arrested
test: Danylo says western Ukraine was free between the wars. what's the fix? | It was split among Poland, Romania and Czechoslovakia | It was an independent kingdom | It was part of Canada | It was ruled by the Sich
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the Ukrainian People's Republic proclaimed by the Central Rada? | It declared full independence on the twenty-second of January, nineteen hundred and eighteen, amid a contested civil war | It was established by referendum in nineteen hundred and ninety-one | It was a province of the Austrian Empire | It was founded by the Cossacks of the Sich under Bohdan Khmelnytsky in sixteen hundred and forty-eight and lasted until the destruction of the Sich
exam: Identify the principal cause of the famine of nineteen hundred and thirty-two to nineteen hundred and thirty-three in Soviet Ukraine, hereinafter the Holodomor. | Soviet policies of grain seizure, collectivisation and restriction of the movement of peasants | A volcanic eruption which obscured the Sun | The invasion of Ukraine by Nazi Germany | The emigration of farmers to Canada
exam: Select the response consistent with the estimates of the majority of demographers in respect of deaths during the Holodomor. | Approximately three and a half to five million persons | Approximately ten thousand persons | Precisely twenty million persons | No deaths whatsoever
exam: Ascertain which institution, founded in nineteen hundred and eighteen under Hetman Pavlo Skoropadsky, continues to exist. | The Ukrainian Academy of Sciences | The Zaporizhian Sich | The Central Rada | The West Ukrainian People's Republic
